 connection loss during the disruption, a different post-restart boot_id (f377f98c...), and full recovery of the account, its resource limits, and overall system health afterward. Lifecycle snapshots corroborate the boot-identity change and show no unwanted residue beyond an expected reboot log artifact.\",\"limitations\":[\"The preparation phase was disabled (before_prepare and after_prepare are identical aliases), so no prepare-vs-executor state separation was needed here.\",\"Node health during the restart window is inferred from connection failures rather than an explicit degraded-state capture, though this is consistent with a genuine reboot.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"account-created","requirement":"Create the appworker account on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Verified no pre-existing appworker user, then created with useradd -m; id confirmed uid=1000(appworker). Account persisted and resolved correctly after the subsequent reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b671e3e85e5d461c","cmd-c99173e0768c4dcd","cmd-8ec25e510ed44895"]},{"id":"resource-limits","requirement":"Limit appworker login sessions to 8,192 open files and 256 processes","status":"satisfied","summary":"Wrote /etc/security/limits.d/appworker.conf with soft/hard nofile 8192 and soft/hard nproc 256, confirmed pam_limits.so active in system-auth, and directly verified via a real su - appworker login session showing ulimit -n=8192 and ulimit -u=256. Re-verified identically after the node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-15b361be88a94763","cmd-3b79865c34624878","cmd-8ec25e510ed44895"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.93,"summary":"Command sequence is tight and purposeful: OS/account check, user creation, limits file write, PAM verification via a real login session, then the mandatory restart-evidence protocol (baseline boot_id/health capture, background systemctl reboot, connection-drop polling, post-restart boot_id and full re-verification of account/limits/system health). No unnecessary or destructive mutations were found; every state-changing command maps directly to satisfying the task or the restart protocol. The only residue is /tmp/reboot.log, an inherent artifact of backgrounding systemctl reboot via nohup redirection — low-risk, expected, and not indicative of abandoned or unsafe state. Failed-services and package-health checks show no damage before or after.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c026c4c330f44dc4","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes of the task — creating the appworker account and constraining its login sessions to 8,192 open files / 256 processes — are directly satisfied with evidence from a real su - login session, not just configuration inspection. The independently required node-restart evidence protocol is also fully and coherently demonstrated: a pre-restart healthy baseline (boot_id 7223d5b7..., systemctl is-system-running=running), an acknowledged systemctl reboot, observed connection loss during the disruption, a different post-restart boot_id (f377f98c...), and full recovery of the account, its resource limits, and overall system health afterward. Lifecycle snapshots corroborate the boot-identity change and show no unwanted residue beyond an expected reboot log artifact.","limitations":["The preparation phase was disabled (before_prepare and after_prepare are identical aliases), so no prepare-vs-executor state separation was needed here.","Node health during the restart window is inferred from connection failures rather than an explicit degraded-state capture, though this is consistent with a genuine reboot."]},"ttft_ms":4685,"type":"result","duration_ms":22569,"uuid":"061db724-5d89-4c76-9dbc-1055a8450f0f","ttft_stream_ms":860,"time_to_request_ms":185,"first_content_frame_ms":861,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
